### What is groupingByConcurrent?

`Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(classifier)` is a concurrent, unordered collector that groups elements into a `ConcurrentMap`, keyed by the classifier. On a parallel stream it lets multiple threads insert into one shared concurrent map simultaneously, avoiding the per-thread-map-then-merge cost that ordinary `groupingBy` incurs in parallel.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class GroupConcurrent {
    record Txn(String accountId, long amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Txn> txns = List.of(
            new Txn("ACC-1", 100), new Txn("ACC-2", 200),
            new Txn("ACC-1", 50),  new Txn("ACC-2", 75));

        ConcurrentMap<String, List<Txn>> byAccount = txns.parallelStream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(Txn::accountId));

        System.out.println(byAccount.keySet());
    }
}
```

```text
[ACC-1, ACC-2]
```

### How does it differ from groupingBy on a parallel stream?

Ordinary `groupingBy` is a non-concurrent collector. On a parallel stream the framework has each thread build its own partial map, then merges those maps at the end. `groupingByConcurrent` is a concurrent collector, so all threads insert into a single shared `ConcurrentMap`, skipping the merge.

| Aspect | `groupingBy` (parallel) | `groupingByConcurrent` (parallel) |
|--------|-------------------------|-----------------------------------|
| Per-thread maps | yes, one per thread | no, one shared map |
| Merge step | yes, merges all partials | no merge needed |
| Map type | `HashMap` | `ConcurrentHashMap` |
| Order within group | preserved | not preserved (unordered) |
| Best when | sequential or ordered groups | large parallel, order irrelevant |

```java
// non-concurrent: per-thread maps merged at the end
Map<String, List<Txn>> a = txns.parallelStream()
    .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingBy(Txn::accountId));

// concurrent: one shared ConcurrentMap, no merge
ConcurrentMap<String, List<Txn>> b = txns.parallelStream()
    .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(Txn::accountId));
```

### Why does the merge step matter for a very large stream?

For millions of transactions, building a HashMap per worker thread and then merging them costs extra memory for the partial maps and extra time to combine them. The concurrent collector removes that overhead by writing to one map, so it scales better when the data is large and the stream is truly parallel.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.LongStream;

public class LargeGrouping {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 5 million synthetic transactions across 1000 accounts
        ConcurrentMap<Long, Long> perAccountCount = LongStream.range(0, 5_000_000L)
            .parallel()
            .boxed()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(
                id -> id % 1000,
                Collectors.counting()));

        System.out.println("accounts = " + perAccountCount.size());
    }
}
```

```text
accounts = 1000
```

### How do you add a downstream collector?

groupingByConcurrent accepts a downstream collector exactly like groupingBy, so you can count, sum, or collect per group. For a total amount per account use a summing collector.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DownstreamConcurrent {
    record Txn(String accountId, long amount) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Txn> txns = List.of(
            new Txn("ACC-1", 100), new Txn("ACC-2", 200),
            new Txn("ACC-1", 50),  new Txn("ACC-2", 75));

        ConcurrentMap<String, Long> totalPerAccount = txns.parallelStream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(
                Txn::accountId,
                Collectors.summingLong(Txn::amount)));

        System.out.println(totalPerAccount);
    }
}
```

```text
{ACC-1=150, ACC-2=275}
```

### What is the unordered trade-off?

groupingByConcurrent is marked UNORDERED, so within each group the encounter order of elements is not preserved. For sums and counts this is irrelevant, but if the order of transactions inside a group matters, the concurrent collector is the wrong choice.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class OrderTradeoff {
    record Txn(String accountId, int seq) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Txn> txns = List.of(
            new Txn("ACC-1", 1), new Txn("ACC-1", 2),
            new Txn("ACC-1", 3), new Txn("ACC-1", 4));

        // ordered: groupingBy preserves encounter order in the list
        Map<String, List<Txn>> ordered = txns.parallelStream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Txn::accountId));

        // unordered: groupingByConcurrent may store them in any order
        ConcurrentMap<String, List<Txn>> unordered = txns.parallelStream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(Txn::accountId));

        System.out.println("ordered   = " + ordered.get("ACC-1"));
        System.out.println("unordered = " + unordered.get("ACC-1"));
    }
}
```

```text
ordered   = [Txn[accountId=ACC-1, seq=1], Txn[accountId=ACC-1, seq=2], Txn[accountId=ACC-1, seq=3], Txn[accountId=ACC-1, seq=4]]
unordered = [Txn[accountId=ACC-1, seq=3], Txn[accountId=ACC-1, seq=1], Txn[accountId=ACC-1, seq=4], Txn[accountId=ACC-1, seq=2]]
```

The unordered list order can vary run to run; only use it when order inside a group does not matter.

### What are common mistakes with groupingByConcurrent?

The common mistakes are expecting it to speed up a sequential stream and assigning the result to a plain HashMap type.

```java
// WRONG: sequential stream gains nothing from groupingByConcurrent
// and pays for concurrent map overhead
ConcurrentMap<String, List<Txn>> slow = txns.stream() // not parallel
    .collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(Txn::accountId));

// WRONG: the result is a ConcurrentMap, not a HashMap
// HashMap<String, List<Txn>> m = txns.parallelStream()
//     .collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(Txn::accountId)); // compile error
```

```java
// CORRECT: parallel stream, result typed as ConcurrentMap or Map
ConcurrentMap<String, List<Txn>> fast = txns.parallelStream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(Txn::accountId));
Map<String, List<Txn>> asMap = fast; // ConcurrentMap is a Map
```

### What are the thread-safety requirements?

Because many threads insert into the shared map at once, the classifier and any downstream collector must be thread-safe and free of shared mutable state. The built-in collectors such as counting and summingLong are safe; a custom downstream that mutates external state is not.

```java
// SAFE: built-in downstream collectors are thread-safe
ConcurrentMap<String, Long> counts = txns.parallelStream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(Txn::accountId, Collectors.counting()));

// UNSAFE: a classifier or downstream that touches shared mutable state races
// int[] calls = {0};
// txns.parallelStream().collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(t -> { calls[0]++; return t.accountId(); }));
```

### When should you use groupingByConcurrent and when not?

Use it when the stream is genuinely parallel, the data set is large enough that merge cost matters, order within groups is irrelevant, and the classifier and downstream are thread-safe. Do not use it for sequential streams, for small data sets where parallel overhead dominates, or when you need the encounter order preserved inside each group. In those cases plain groupingBy is correct.

```java
// use groupingByConcurrent: huge parallel count, order irrelevant
ConcurrentMap<String, Long> perAccount = bigTxns.parallelStream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingByConcurrent(Txn::accountId, Collectors.counting()));

// use groupingBy: sequential, or ordered groups needed
Map<String, List<Txn>> ordered = txns.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Txn::accountId));
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: How is groupingByConcurrent different from groupingBy in parallel? Answer: it fills one shared ConcurrentMap with no per-thread merge, while groupingBy builds and merges partial maps. What is the trade-off? Answer: it is unordered, so encounter order within a group is not preserved. What map type does it return? Answer: a ConcurrentMap, typically ConcurrentHashMap. Does it help a sequential stream? Answer: no, it only pays off when the stream is genuinely parallel and large. What must be thread-safe? Answer: the classifier and any downstream collector, because many threads write to the shared map at once.
